WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:04.073
We have a lot to learn from science and
from medicine.

00:00:04.073 --> 00:00:09.273
Yet sometimes it can be difficult to
understand exactly what we know and

00:00:09.273 --> 00:00:10.841
what we need to know.

00:00:10.841 --> 00:00:12.186
I'm Matt Abrahams and

00:00:12.186 --> 00:00:16.727
I teach Strategic Communication at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:16.727 --> 00:00:20.824
Welcome to Think Fast Talk Smart,
The Podcast.

00:00:20.824 --> 00:00:25.212
Today I am super excited to
chat with Dr Lloyd Minor,

00:00:25.212 --> 00:00:28.715
Dean of the Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:28.715 --> 00:00:32.138
Lloyd is a scientist,
surgeon, and academic leader.

00:00:32.138 --> 00:00:33.090
He is the Carl and

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:37.254
Elizabeth Naumann Dean of the Stanford
University School of Medicine.

00:00:37.254 --> 00:00:40.352
He is also a Professor of Head and
Neck Surgery, and

00:00:40.352 --> 00:00:43.459
a Professor of Bioengineering and
Neurobiology.

00:00:43.459 --> 00:00:48.200
In 2020, he wrote the book
Discovering Precision Health,

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:52.075
and he is also the host of
the Minor Consult podcast.

00:00:52.075 --> 00:00:56.168
Well, hi, Lloyd, I'm really excited
to have you on the podcast and

00:00:56.168 --> 00:00:58.294
look forward to our conversation.

00:00:58.294 --> 00:01:00.374
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Matt,
I'm delighted to be here and

00:01:00.374 --> 00:01:02.146
I too look forward to our conversation.

00:01:02.146 --> 00:01:04.550
&gt;&gt; Great, let's go ahead and get started.

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:09.091
First, to begin, congratulations on
your podcast, The Minor Consult.

00:01:09.091 --> 00:01:11.574
I've listened in, and
I enjoy it very much.

00:01:11.574 --> 00:01:12.396
I'm curious,

00:01:12.396 --> 00:01:16.389
what led you to host a podcast and
what are you hoping to accomplish with it?

00:01:16.389 --> 00:01:21.282
&gt;&gt; In the early days of COVID-19 when
there was a lot of information coming out,

00:01:21.282 --> 00:01:24.725
a lot of science being done,
a lot that we didn't know.

00:01:24.725 --> 00:01:30.003
I started doing some media appearances
really to try to explain what we do know

00:01:30.003 --> 00:01:35.376
and what we don't know, to serve,
I hope, as a trusted information source.

00:01:35.376 --> 00:01:40.013
And I enjoyed doing that and got good
feedback on it and really continued to do

00:01:40.013 --> 00:01:44.447
that throughout the pandemic,
not that we're out of the pandemic yet.

00:01:44.447 --> 00:01:48.596
But throughout the, shall we say,
the pre-vaccination stage and

00:01:48.596 --> 00:01:51.088
vaccine rollout stage of the pandemic.

00:01:51.088 --> 00:01:56.406
And so I was looking for a way to
continue that process of communicating

00:01:56.406 --> 00:02:01.093
with a large group of people,
anyone who wants to listen, and

00:02:01.093 --> 00:02:05.799
hopefully through the podcast,
also learn from the guests.

00:02:05.799 --> 00:02:08.016
The podcast is about leadership.

00:02:08.016 --> 00:02:10.696
It's about leadership in a crisis.

00:02:10.696 --> 00:02:15.946
And I've had the privilege of interviewing
guests from different professions and

00:02:15.946 --> 00:02:16.775
interests.

00:02:16.775 --> 00:02:17.824
And I've learned a lot from it.

00:02:17.824 --> 00:02:19.953
Hopefully the listeners have as well.

00:02:19.953 --> 00:02:23.828
I think one of the central
messages of the pandemic to me and

00:02:23.828 --> 00:02:29.220
I'll be processing these past two plus
years for the next how many, every year?

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.316
&gt;&gt; I think we all will, yeah.

00:02:30.316 --> 00:02:34.839
Yeah, but one of the central messages is,
we need to be more thoughtful about,

00:02:34.839 --> 00:02:37.281
more engaged with the way we communicate.

00:02:37.281 --> 00:02:41.183
I think that's particularly
true in the science and

00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:46.149
medical communities,
where I don't think our communication has

00:02:46.149 --> 00:02:51.044
always served the public well or
the scientific interests well.

00:02:51.044 --> 00:02:52.961
&gt;&gt; I hope we can start
that conversation today,

00:02:52.961 --> 00:02:54.835
given that we focus
a lot on communication.

00:02:54.835 --> 00:02:58.763
And I just want to give you
a personal nod and thank you.

00:02:58.763 --> 00:03:02.385
During the pandemic,
you certainly provided a steady hand and

00:03:02.385 --> 00:03:06.757
some good clear advice during times
of confusion, both for me personally,

00:03:06.757 --> 00:03:11.226
I know for my colleagues and on the
campus, and actually in our general area.

00:03:11.226 --> 00:03:12.020
So thank you for

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:15.758
that, and I'll just echo hosting
a podcast is a fantastic way to learn.

00:03:15.758 --> 00:03:19.595
I have learned so much and I am sure
I will learn a ton from you as well.

00:03:19.595 --> 00:03:23.631
Speaking of learning, I recently learned
that you consider yourself an introvert.

00:03:23.631 --> 00:03:29.317
I'm very curious to learn how your
introversion has helped you be successful.

00:03:29.317 --> 00:03:32.800
And further, I'd love to hear your advice,
tips or tricks you have for

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:35.777
many of our listeners who
consider themselves introverts.

00:03:35.777 --> 00:03:38.295
And for
those of us who might not be introverted,

00:03:38.295 --> 00:03:41.559
what we could do to help our
colleagues and our friends who are.

00:03:41.559 --> 00:03:45.846
&gt;&gt; If one adopts the definition of
introvert, extrovert is in terms of how

00:03:45.846 --> 00:03:51.218
you recharge, so, classically, extroverts
recharge by being immersed with others.

00:03:51.218 --> 00:03:55.074
And introverts recharge by
being with themselves or

00:03:55.074 --> 00:03:59.014
a very small group of people or
person that they have as

00:03:59.014 --> 00:04:03.850
a particularly trusted adviser or
spouse, in my case, my wife.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:08.177
And I think that that describes my
personality in terms of how I recharge.

00:04:08.177 --> 00:04:14.162
Now, what I've learned about myself
in leadership in particular is,

00:04:14.162 --> 00:04:19.346
one of the reasons I feel like I
have the best job on the planet is

00:04:19.346 --> 00:04:24.532
that I interact with such a group
of amazingly bright people on

00:04:24.532 --> 00:04:31.146
a daily basis through what I do at
Stanford University and Stanford Medicine.

00:04:31.146 --> 00:04:33.551
And then more broadly as
a representative of Stanford and

00:04:33.551 --> 00:04:34.763
as a leader in biomedicine.

00:04:34.763 --> 00:04:39.720
And I derive a lot of energy
from those interactions and

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.322
from learning from others.

00:04:42.322 --> 00:04:46.402
But in order to process and
really sort of plan,

00:04:46.402 --> 00:04:49.360
then I need to have time to think and

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:54.681
sort things out individually or
with a small group of others.

00:04:54.681 --> 00:04:56.145
&gt;&gt; Thank you for sharing that.

00:04:56.145 --> 00:05:00.650
I think for those who listen and who are
introverted, to hear that their approach

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:04.837
is equally valuable and in some cases
more valuable can be very affirming.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:09.053
And to recognize that we need both types
of people, those who are extroverted and

00:05:09.053 --> 00:05:13.405
I fall into that category, and those who
are introverted to make things work well.

00:05:13.405 --> 00:05:17.324
You mentioned listening in your response
to my question about introversion and

00:05:17.324 --> 00:05:19.613
I was really struck by
a quote I heard of yours.

00:05:19.613 --> 00:05:24.316
It went, one of the most active things
that I do is to listen, to process what

00:05:24.316 --> 00:05:29.040
I'm hearing and to come up with informed
questions that bring us together.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:32.484
Can you share your thoughts on
the importance of listening beyond

00:05:32.484 --> 00:05:35.302
what you've already shared
around introversion and

00:05:35.302 --> 00:05:39.009
provide us with any best practices
you personally use to listen well.

00:05:39.009 --> 00:05:43.544
&gt;&gt; Sure, I think the most valuable
clerkship that I had in medical

00:05:43.544 --> 00:05:46.293
school was my psychiatry clerkship.

00:05:46.293 --> 00:05:49.931
And it's because of something
that our preceptor did for

00:05:49.931 --> 00:05:54.042
the two of us students rotating
with our preceptor at that time.

00:05:54.042 --> 00:05:57.058
And it was a six week rotation.

00:05:57.058 --> 00:06:03.407
For every day, at least three days a week,
each of us would interview a patient.

00:06:03.407 --> 00:06:08.032
We weren't allowed to take notes, and
the other person would be listening.

00:06:08.032 --> 00:06:12.512
And after the interview,
each of us independently would write,

00:06:12.512 --> 00:06:18.133
we were asked to write verbatim what we
heard during that 30 minute interview and

00:06:18.133 --> 00:06:20.265
discussion with the patient.

00:06:20.265 --> 00:06:23.589
And at first,
it was extraordinarily difficult, I mean,

00:06:23.589 --> 00:06:25.992
it was just the hardest
thing in the world.

00:06:25.992 --> 00:06:32.193
But it became, I wouldn't say easy, but it
became easier with time of the experience.

00:06:32.193 --> 00:06:35.725
And the point is,
you really have to concentrate and

00:06:35.725 --> 00:06:38.544
focus if you're going to really listen.

00:06:38.544 --> 00:06:43.045
And I've tried to deploy that in
the other things I've done as a surgeon,

00:06:43.045 --> 00:06:45.962
scientist, as a leader,
it really does help.

00:06:45.962 --> 00:06:50.246
First, it helps to build trust in others
if they really can see that you're

00:06:50.246 --> 00:06:51.003
listening.

00:06:51.003 --> 00:06:56.783
It helps to really have a dialogue if
in addition to what you're saying,

00:06:56.783 --> 00:07:00.311
you're actually heading what others say.

00:07:00.311 --> 00:07:03.796
And that comes back in the way
you converse with them,

00:07:03.796 --> 00:07:06.906
that they know that
you're listening to them.

00:07:06.906 --> 00:07:11.575
I've always emphasized that I think
listening is the most important trait and

00:07:11.575 --> 00:07:13.627
characteristic of leadership and

00:07:13.627 --> 00:07:17.174
undergirds the success in every
other area of leadership.

00:07:17.174 --> 00:07:21.101
And I don't think it receives enough
attention as we're talking about

00:07:21.101 --> 00:07:22.416
leadership training.

00:07:22.416 --> 00:07:26.198
And as we're talking about communication,
certainly what we say is important.

00:07:26.198 --> 00:07:30.974
But it's even more important that what we
are saying is based upon what we're taking

00:07:30.974 --> 00:07:32.663
in from listening to others.

00:07:32.663 --> 00:07:36.139
&gt;&gt; So what I'm hearing you say really is
that you need to take the time to listen,

00:07:36.139 --> 00:07:37.814
really focus and concentrate on it.

00:07:37.814 --> 00:07:41.630
It is an important and
an act that requires a lot of attention.

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:45.094
And it can really breed connection and
validate others,

00:07:45.094 --> 00:07:47.922
and that's an important
part of leadership.

00:07:47.922 --> 00:07:50.868
And I agree,
as somebody who teaches communication,

00:07:50.868 --> 00:07:53.620
a lot of what gets taught is,
how to say your piece,

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:57.221
not necessarily how to listen to
what the other people are saying.

00:07:57.221 --> 00:08:00.653
During your tenure as the Dean of
the Stanford School of Medicine,

00:08:00.653 --> 00:08:04.834
you've had to address many significant
issues happening on campus and beyond.

00:08:04.834 --> 00:08:09.006
What are your thoughts on the role of
leaders and communication in times of

00:08:09.006 --> 00:08:12.653
ambiguity and challenge,
any insights that you've learned?

00:08:12.653 --> 00:08:16.980
&gt;&gt; I think one insight is when someone
doesn't know the answer to a question, or

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.977
when they answer to the question
is simply not known,

00:08:19.977 --> 00:08:21.921
we need to say that we don't know.

00:08:21.921 --> 00:08:25.894
And I think a lot of experts
early on in the pandemic,

00:08:25.894 --> 00:08:30.875
that was problematic because
that really wasn't being stated.

00:08:30.875 --> 00:08:33.262
And then there was so much we didn't know.

00:08:33.262 --> 00:08:38.615
And in a sense, it's not reassuring,
but it's far more of a problem if

00:08:38.615 --> 00:08:43.717
we try to pretend we do, and
then later on, of course, we didn't.

00:08:43.717 --> 00:08:47.625
So I think being honest,
being forthright, following through,

00:08:47.625 --> 00:08:52.577
if it's a specific question I simply don't
know the answer to, but the answer is out

00:08:52.577 --> 00:08:57.078
there, then I should be finding out
the answer and then relaying that back.

00:08:57.078 --> 00:09:01.001
The idea is rather than
pushing ambiguity away,

00:09:01.001 --> 00:09:05.209
we should lean into it and
use it as a stimulus to guide

00:09:05.209 --> 00:09:10.101
our communication in more effective ways
&gt;&gt; One of the things that I really

00:09:10.101 --> 00:09:14.084
respected about you and some of your
colleagues who became front and center in

00:09:14.084 --> 00:09:18.126
the news on a regular basis was the level
of transparency that you would admit,

00:09:18.126 --> 00:09:19.006
we don't know.

00:09:19.006 --> 00:09:22.714
Or we're learning, or we said this
one day and now we know this as well.

00:09:22.714 --> 00:09:25.889
So, in addition to saying I don't know,

00:09:25.889 --> 00:09:30.721
I think it's important to put
a focus on transparency as well.

00:09:30.721 --> 00:09:33.871
And I saw that played out
often in your communication.

00:09:33.871 --> 00:09:37.217
I've heard you talk about
authentic leadership.

00:09:37.217 --> 00:09:41.079
Can you tell us what you mean by that and
why is this type of leadership so

00:09:41.079 --> 00:09:42.133
important to you?

00:09:42.133 --> 00:09:45.872
&gt;&gt; To me,
authenticity means being forthright,

00:09:45.872 --> 00:09:49.334
being honest, being empathic, for sure.

00:09:49.334 --> 00:09:52.976
It means putting yourself in
the shoes of the other person.

00:09:52.976 --> 00:09:55.411
It means earning the right to be a leader,

00:09:55.411 --> 00:10:00.361
which is something it doesn't come with
a title, it doesn't come with resources.

00:10:00.361 --> 00:10:04.037
To be an effective leader,
you need to earn that right,

00:10:04.037 --> 00:10:08.508
that privilege, that trust from
the people that you're leading.

00:10:08.508 --> 00:10:11.046
And they look to you for leadership.

00:10:11.046 --> 00:10:15.842
I think authenticity is something today
that particularly given the impact of

00:10:15.842 --> 00:10:18.833
social media and
the immediacy of social media.

00:10:18.833 --> 00:10:24.246
Oftentimes, authenticity is not
represented [LAUGH] in a soundbite or

00:10:24.246 --> 00:10:25.170
in a tweet.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:29.289
In fact, if anything,
it gets us further away from authenticity.

00:10:29.289 --> 00:10:35.091
I think there's a basic yearning today
in society to have some bedrock truths,

00:10:35.091 --> 00:10:40.721
to understand our differences better
than we do today, and to be authentic,

00:10:40.721 --> 00:10:45.160
expressing those differences and
expressing those views.

00:10:45.160 --> 00:10:49.257
But hopefully from the authenticity when
it exists on both sides of an issue,

00:10:49.257 --> 00:10:51.085
when that authenticity is there,

00:10:51.085 --> 00:10:55.008
that will actually help people work
together even though they disagree.

00:10:55.008 --> 00:10:57.780
And I think we're sorely
lacking that right now.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:00.622
&gt;&gt; I'm curious as you think
about authentic leadership,

00:11:00.622 --> 00:11:02.201
what's the role of feedback?

00:11:02.201 --> 00:11:05.279
It seems to me that feedback would
be an important part of that,

00:11:05.279 --> 00:11:07.091
I'm curious how that works for you.

00:11:07.091 --> 00:11:09.336
&gt;&gt; It's essential to building trust.

00:11:09.336 --> 00:11:14.939
Another trait that helps in
building trust is to be vulnerable.

00:11:14.939 --> 00:11:17.637
And goodness knows,
we were, all of us, but

00:11:17.637 --> 00:11:22.252
particularly those of us in leadership,
have throughout COVID, whether or

00:11:22.252 --> 00:11:26.732
not we acknowledged it or not,
we've been extraordinarily vulnerable.

00:11:26.732 --> 00:11:31.439
And I think to acknowledge that,
look, we may not get this right and

00:11:31.439 --> 00:11:35.734
we depend upon your feedback to
recalibrate and get it right,

00:11:35.734 --> 00:11:38.719
that that's just absolutely essential.

00:11:38.719 --> 00:11:42.150
By discipline,
I'm trained as a neurosurgeon,

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:47.311
as a lateral skull-based surgeon,
and those are highly precise fields.

00:11:47.311 --> 00:11:51.013
And when I became provost at Johns
Hopkins, I stopped operating because if

00:11:51.013 --> 00:11:54.083
you're not doing it regularly,
you shouldn't be doing it.

00:11:54.083 --> 00:11:58.416
The point is that your best
opportunity to do good for

00:11:58.416 --> 00:12:02.552
your patient is the first
time you do something and

00:12:02.552 --> 00:12:06.602
do it absolutely textbook
right the first time.

00:12:06.602 --> 00:12:12.577
And there is a clear right and wrong
with most things in my clinical field.

00:12:12.577 --> 00:12:15.287
Leadership is very,
very different from that.

00:12:15.287 --> 00:12:18.599
I find myself rarely do I
get it right the first time.

00:12:18.599 --> 00:12:23.161
I depend upon the feedback of others,
unless this is a critically important

00:12:23.161 --> 00:12:27.950
time-sensitive decision, and we were
faced with many of those during COVID.

00:12:27.950 --> 00:12:32.874
In those cases, you do the best you
can and you recalibrate very quickly.

00:12:32.874 --> 00:12:38.192
In cases that are not as acute
as they have been during COVID,

00:12:38.192 --> 00:12:42.780
you can put up a straw person,
you can float an idea,

00:12:42.780 --> 00:12:47.369
you can express your opinion and
your belief about,

00:12:47.369 --> 00:12:50.521
this is the way I think we should go.

00:12:50.521 --> 00:12:52.425
Some of that will be embraced,
some of it won't.

00:12:52.425 --> 00:12:53.728
But if you take the feedback,

00:12:53.728 --> 00:12:56.087
it's not going to affect your
credibility as a leader.

00:12:56.087 --> 00:12:58.253
It's very different than
a surgical procedure.

00:12:58.253 --> 00:13:01.718
You're going to tell a patient
before you do a surgical procedure,

00:13:01.718 --> 00:13:05.309
I think I'm going to do it this way today,
but that may not work, and so

00:13:05.309 --> 00:13:08.470
we'll then do something
different tomorrow or next week.

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:10.419
I mean, no one wants to hear that-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Right.

00:13:10.419 --> 00:13:16.398
&gt;&gt; And for me,
that was a recalibration as a leader.

00:13:16.398 --> 00:13:18.303
I feel really fortunate to have had
kind of both sides represented.

00:13:18.303 --> 00:13:23.324
And I think it's made me able to act
definitively when the circumstance

00:13:23.324 --> 00:13:28.190
requires it and when people around
me expect me to act definitively.

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:31.458
And also to be more graduated and

00:13:31.458 --> 00:13:37.516
incremental when the situation
justifies that approach.

00:13:37.516 --> 00:13:42.168
&gt;&gt; I think it's great that you take the
time to reflect on feedback and to be so

00:13:42.168 --> 00:13:42.928
open to it.

00:13:42.928 --> 00:13:43.769
Before we end,

00:13:43.769 --> 00:13:47.861
I'd like to ask you the same three
questions I asked everyone who joins me.

00:13:47.861 --> 00:13:48.948
Are you up for that?

00:13:48.948 --> 00:13:49.728
&gt;&gt; I sure am.

00:13:49.728 --> 00:13:50.624
&gt;&gt; Excellent,

00:13:50.624 --> 00:13:55.593
if you were to capture the best
communication advice you ever received as

00:13:55.593 --> 00:14:00.246
a five to seven word presentation
slide title, what would that be?

00:14:00.246 --> 00:14:04.197
&gt;&gt; Empathic listening for
impactful leadership.

00:14:04.197 --> 00:14:06.713
&gt;&gt; I like it, and
it even has some rhyming to it.

00:14:06.713 --> 00:14:09.113
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Question number two,

00:14:09.113 --> 00:14:11.699
who is a communicator that you admire and
why?

00:14:11.699 --> 00:14:15.201
&gt;&gt; Ruth Simmons,
Former President of Brown University,

00:14:15.201 --> 00:14:17.815
Current President of Prairie View A&amp;M.

00:14:17.815 --> 00:14:21.355
I got to know Ruth when she was
president of Brown as a Brown alum.

00:14:21.355 --> 00:14:25.955
Ruth asked me to serve on a corporation
committee on medical education many

00:14:25.955 --> 00:14:26.692
years ago.

00:14:26.692 --> 00:14:33.983
And I think Ruth's ability to lean
into complex controversial topics,

00:14:33.983 --> 00:14:38.762
and through the strength
of her personality and

00:14:38.762 --> 00:14:44.498
her intellect really get people
to do their best thinking

00:14:44.498 --> 00:14:49.658
around an issue and
come up with recommendations.

00:14:49.658 --> 00:14:51.702
I think she's extraordinary at that.

00:14:51.702 --> 00:14:52.789
&gt;&gt; Final question,

00:14:52.789 --> 00:14:58.090
what are the first three ingredients that
go into a successful communication recipe?

00:14:58.090 --> 00:15:02.918
&gt;&gt; Listen, learn, recalibrate,
I think to learn, you have to listen,

00:15:02.918 --> 00:15:05.422
and we talked a lot about that today.

00:15:05.422 --> 00:15:10.023
But you also had to be willing to say,
look, I probably got that wrong before,

00:15:10.023 --> 00:15:13.719
and you've got to be willing to
say that first of yourself, and

00:15:13.719 --> 00:15:16.830
then you've got to be willing
to say that to others.

00:15:16.830 --> 00:15:20.345
And then seek the input from
others that help you and

00:15:20.345 --> 00:15:22.894
others get it right moving forward.

00:15:22.894 --> 00:15:26.155
Well, Dean Minor, thank you so
much for being here.

00:15:26.155 --> 00:15:30.827
The work you and all of your colleagues
do at the Stanford Medical School is so

00:15:30.827 --> 00:15:31.631
important.

00:15:31.631 --> 00:15:35.019
And a personal thank you for
all that you and

00:15:35.019 --> 00:15:38.324
everyone there did during the pandemic.

00:15:38.324 --> 00:15:41.095
I appreciate your time and
your thoughtful and

00:15:41.095 --> 00:15:45.432
thought provoking ideas and
actions around effective leadership, and

00:15:45.432 --> 00:15:49.003
the importance of communication
in science and medicine.

00:15:49.003 --> 00:15:52.757
Finally, best of luck on your podcast,
the Minor Consult.

00:15:52.757 --> 00:15:57.574
Everyone listening in has a lot to
learn from you and your guests.

00:15:57.574 --> 00:15:58.510
&gt;&gt; Thanks a lot, Matt.

